{"fudge":{"id":"1ddc6a4d-15db-4870-9054-27678d52c7f3","word":"fudge","trans":[{"lang":"Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"euphemism for \"fuck!\"","word":"fosca-se!"}],"pos":"intj","tags":null,"senses":[{"examples":[{"text":"Fudge! if you had such a fine instinct, why did you let us go to Transome Court and make fools of ourselves?","ref":"1866, George Eliot, Felix Holt:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[0,5]],"id":"a01ecdb8-cc67-4aaa-9f35-91fc383d62b2"},{"text":"Pearl had to go home. Mike was one of the last of the good guys, so he said he'd run her on home. All Robert could say was, \"Ah, fudge.\"","ref":"1966, Bruce Brown, director, The Endless Summer:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[129,134]],"id":"8488d856-195e-48b3-88b7-50b94b4304b9"},{"text":"Sabrina was no longer in the witch’s grasp; in fact, she was staring directly at the woman’s crusty, corny feet. Fudge, I made her a giant, Sabrina thought to herself […]","ref":"2006, Michael Buckley, chapter 9, in The Problem Child (The Sisters Grimm; 3), New York, N.Y.: Amulet Books, →ISBN, page 235:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[113,118]],"id":"c966191d-c511-43d4-b175-ef3a9901481e"}],"categories":["English colloquialisms","English minced oaths","English terms with quotations"],"links":[["minced oath","minced oath"],["fuck","fuck#English"]],"raw_glosses":["(colloquial, minced oath) Used in place of fuck."],"qualifier":"minced oath","glosses":["Used in place of fuck."],"tags":["colloquial"],"id":"af4ab5c0-4e06-47a9-8030-0ef8b3419b4a"},{"examples":[{"text":"Oh, fudge! Don't lecture me.","ref":"1871, George Eliot, Middlemarch:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[4,9]],"id":"f75b2f28-e1ad-4946-b466-b58164b31d9d"}],"categories":["English colloquialisms","English terms with archaic senses","English terms with quotations"],"links":[["Nonsense","nonsense"],["tommyrot","tommyrot"]],"raw_glosses":["(colloquial, archaic) Nonsense; tommyrot."],"glosses":["Nonsense; tommyrot."],"t